==== exit ====
0
==== stdout ====
raphael - an actionable second brain for agents

Usage: raphael <command> [options]

Start here:
  1. Make a private key file:
     `[ -e .env ] && echo ".env already exists; add RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY to it yourself." >&2 || (umask 077 && set -C && echo "RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > .env)`
  2. Run `raphael server serve` in that directory; keep that terminal open.
  3. In another terminal run `raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000` and enter the
     key from .env.
  4. Run `raphael status`, then `raphael list /`.
  5. Run `raphael create resource.note /work/first-note --body 'My first note'`.
  Already have a server? Start at step 3 with its address and key. See
  `raphael help connecting`.

Read:
  favorites          List favorites by title.
  get                Read one item and its current revision.
  path               Print an item’s current full path.
  list               List children of one or more paths or ids.
  search             Search all descendants by text.
  help               Read command help and cross-command guidance.

Write:
  create             Create an area, project, or note.
  update             Change an item with revision protection.
  move               Move an item while keeping its id.
  archive            Hide an item and everything inside it.
  restore            Remove an item’s own archive cause.
  favorite           Add an area or project to favorites.
  unfavorite         Remove an area or project from favorites.

Setup:
  status             Check the connection: which server, from where, and whether
                     it accepts the key.
  login              Save a verified server address and API key on this machine.
  logout             Remove the saved login from this machine.
  server             Manage the server on this machine.
  server serve       Run a Raphael server on this machine.
  version            Print the installed Raphael release.

What things are:
  An area holds ongoing work. A project holds work with an outcome. A note holds
    content.
  Types: area, project, resource.note. Items that can hold children: area,
    project.
  The root (/) can directly hold: area.
  An area can directly hold: area, project, resource.note.
  A project can directly hold: resource.note.
  A resource.note cannot hold children.
  Address an item by absolute path or stable id; a revision protects a write
    from concurrent edits.

Topics:
  addressing         Paths, ids, slugs, containment and field limits.
  content            Note bodies, files, stdin and Markdown conversion.
  revisions          Safe writes, conflicts, archives and unknown outcomes.
  output             Text results, JSON envelopes, paging and diagnostics.
  connecting         Start a local server and connect a client.
  exit-codes         Choose recovery from the process exit code.
  reference          All command pages and topics in one stream.
  Use `raphael help <command-or-topic>`, or `raphael <command> --help`.
  `raphael help --json` prints machine-readable definitions.

Examples:
  raphael login
    Connect using the server address and key.
  raphael create resource.note /work/first-note --body 'My first note'
    Write a note in the initial work area.
  raphael list /
    Read the top level.

Options:
  -h, --help    Show help.
      --version Show the version.

Exit codes:
  0    Done. This includes no-ops and success with warnings.
  1    Failed for another reason, with nothing left uncertain.
  2    The command as typed can never succeed; nothing was applied.
  3    Not found.
  4    Conflicts with stored state; nothing was applied.
  5    Not configured, or not authorized.
  6    Not applied, and retrying may work.
  7    Outcome unknown: a write was sent and no definite answer came back.
  130  Interrupted by SIGINT.
  143  Interrupted by SIGTERM.

---

Usage: raphael create <type> <path> [options]
       raphael create <type> --parent-id <id> --slug <slug> [options]

Create an area, project, or note.

Types: area, project, resource.note. A full path supplies both parent and slug.
The parent must already exist.

Areas and projects require --title. Without --title, a note takes its title from
the text of the body's first paragraph or heading, with its line breaks read as
spaces, then from the description's first line, cut at 200 characters. Metadata
is set once, at create.

Rules:
  See `raphael help addressing` for containment and slug rules. A taken slug,
    including one reserved by an archived item, is refused.
  When supplying a body, give either --body or --body-literal. Give each tag its
    own --tag.
  A value that starts with -, such as a Markdown list, must be joined to its
    flag with =, as in `--body='- first item'`; otherwise it is read as another
    option and refused. A longer body is easier as a file or a pipe:
    `--body @list.md` or `--body @-`.

Options:
      --title <text>
          Name, at most 200 characters.
      --parent-id <id>
          Parent id instead of a full path. Requires --slug.
      --slug <slug>
          Address within the parent, at most 100 characters. See
          `raphael help addressing`.
      --description <text>
          Description, at most 4000 characters.
      --tag <tag>
          Repeat for each tag; at most 25 tags of 50 characters. Commas are
          refused. Repeatable.
      --metadata <json|@file>
          JSON object set once at create. @- is refused.
      --body <text|@file|@->
          Body text, @file, or @- for stdin.
      --body-literal <text>
          Literal body text, including text beginning with @.
      --body-format <format>
          Submitted body: markdown or tiptap. Default: markdown.
      --strict
          Refuse a Markdown body with constructs Raphael cannot store as
          written. See `raphael help content`.
      --return-format <format>
          Returned body: markdown or tiptap. Requires --json. Default: markdown.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael create area /work/clients --title Clients
  raphael create project /work/raphael --title Raphael
  raphael create resource.note /work/raphael/api-design --body '# API design'
  raphael create resource.note --parent-id 42 --slug review --title Review --tag reviewed

Prints:
  The created item with its id, path and revision. A warning on stderr names
  Markdown not stored as written. JSON carries `entity`.

Next: raphael list / -r

See also: raphael help addressing; raphael help content; raphael help update

---

Usage: raphael update <path> [changes] [options]
       raphael update --id <id> [changes] [options]

Change an item with revision protection.

Give at least one change. Tags replace the complete tag list; --no-tags clears
it. A renamed title does not change the path.

A body replacement requires the revision you read. Other changes may read and
pin the current id and revision for you. Conflicts are never retried
automatically.

Rules:
  Metadata is set once, at create. --active and --inactive apply only to
    projects.
  On a revision conflict, get the item again and reconcile your intended change
    before updating with its new revision.
  A value that starts with -, such as a Markdown list, must be joined to its
    flag with =, as in `--body='- first item'`; otherwise it is read as another
    option and refused. A longer body is easier as a file or a pipe:
    `--body @list.md` or `--body @-`.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Address by stable id instead of path.
      --title <text>
          Name, at most 200 characters.
      --description <text>
          Description, at most 4000 characters. An empty string clears it.
      --slug <slug>
          Address within the parent, at most 100 characters. See
          `raphael help addressing`.
      --body <text|@file|@->
          Body text, @file, or @- for stdin. An empty string clears it. Requires
          --revision.
      --body-literal <text>
          Literal body text, including text beginning with @. Requires
          --revision.
      --body-format <format>
          Submitted body: markdown or tiptap. Default: markdown.
      --strict
          Refuse a Markdown body with constructs Raphael cannot store as
          written. See `raphael help content`.
      --tag <tag>
          Repeat for each tag; at most 25 tags of 50 characters. Commas are
          refused. Replaces all tags. Repeatable.
      --no-tags
          Clear all tags.
      --active
          Mark the project active.
      --inactive
          Mark the project inactive.
      --revision <n>
          Revision read previously. Required to replace the body; for other
          changes, an omitted revision is read just before sending.
      --return-format <format>
          Returned body: markdown or tiptap. Requires --json. Default: markdown.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael update /work/raphael/api-design --body '# Revised design' --revision 8
  raphael update /work/raphael --tag reviewed --tag contracts
  raphael update --id 42 --title 'Contract review' --description ''

Prints:
  The item with its path and revision, and the path it had when --slug changed
  it. When every value given matches what is stored it reads Unchanged and the
  revision is kept. JSON carries `entity`, `changed` and `previousPath`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help revisions; raphael help content

---

Usage: raphael move <path> <destination> [options]
       raphael move --id <id> <destination> [options]

Move an item while keeping its id.

An existing destination area or project receives the item with its current slug.
Otherwise the last path segment supplies a new slug and its parent must exist.

Everything inside moves with it. Nothing is overwritten. A directly archived
item must be restored first; an item archived only through a parent can move to
an active parent.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Address by stable id instead of path.
      --revision <n>
          Revision read previously. When omitted, it is read just before
          sending.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael move /work/raphael /engineering
  raphael move /work/raphael /engineering/platform
  raphael move --id 42 /

Prints:
  The item with its new path, the path it had, and its revision; Unchanged when
  it was already there. JSON carries `node`, `changed` and `previousPath`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help addressing; raphael help revisions

---

Usage: raphael archive <path> [options]
       raphael archive --id <id> [options]

Hide an item and everything inside it.

Archive hides items from ordinary lists and search without deleting them. The
slug stays reserved, and favorites and project active state are retained.

Archiving an item already hidden through a parent adds its own archive cause.
Restoring the parent then leaves this item archived. There is no delete command.

Archive causes print one per line, nearest first: directly, or through the
archived container above the item, then who set the cause in parentheses. (user)
is a cause set with archive; restore removes only that cause, and only on the
item itself. JSON carries each cause as origin, owner and reason.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Address by stable id instead of path.
      --revision <n>
          Revision read previously. When omitted, it is read just before
          sending.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael archive /work/raphael
  raphael archive --id 42
  raphael archive --id 42 --revision 8 --json

Prints:
  The item with its path, revision and archive causes; Unchanged when it was
  already archived. JSON carries `node`, `archiveCauses` and `changed`.

Next: raphael restore --id 42

See also: raphael help restore; raphael help revisions

---

Usage: raphael restore <path> [options]
       raphael restore --id <id> [options]

Remove an item’s own archive cause.

Restore removes only the item’s own archive cause. If a parent remains archived,
the item stays archived and the answer names the parent to restore.

An already active item succeeds without a change.

Archive causes print one per line, nearest first: directly, or through the
archived container above the item, then who set the cause in parentheses. (user)
is a cause set with archive; restore removes only that cause, and only on the
item itself. JSON carries each cause as origin, owner and reason.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Address by stable id instead of path.
      --revision <n>
          Revision read previously. When omitted, it is read just before
          sending.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael restore /work/raphael
  raphael restore --id 42
  raphael restore --id 42 --revision 8 --json

Prints:
  The item with its path, revision and remaining archive causes. Still archived,
  with the restore command for the container, when a container keeps it
  archived; Unchanged when nothing was removed. JSON carries `node`,
  `archiveCauses` and `changed`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help archive; raphael help revisions

