Usage: raphael list <path>... [options]
       raphael list --id <id> [options]

List children of one or more paths or ids.

Use / for the root. Mix paths and repeated --id, up to 10 distinct places. A
note has no children and returns an empty page.

Direct children are listed by default. -r includes all descendants. Ordering
applies before paging.

Rules:
  The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip
    skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one
    request.
  --order-by accepts slug, updatedAt, id with asc or desc; for example
    `--order-by updatedAt:desc`.
  --filter accepts a JSON object with keys type, kind, tags. Allowed type
    values: area, project, resource. Allowed kind values: note. Each value is a
    scalar or {"$in":[...]}; sibling keys are ANDed. Tags match at least one
    supplied tag, with exact case after trimming and NFC normalization. A kind
    does not imply a type.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Include this id alongside positional paths. Repeatable.
      --filter <json|@file>
          JSON object. Keys: type, kind, tags. Values may use {"$in":[...]}. See
          `raphael help addressing`.
      --skip <n>
          Number of results to skip. Default: 0.
      --limit <n>
          Page size, 1 to 500. Default: 50.
      --include-archived
          Include archived items.
  -r, --recursive
          Include all descendants.
      --order-by <field:direction>
          Repeat in priority order. Fields: slug, updatedAt, id. Directions:
          asc, desc. Repeatable. Default: slug:asc then id:asc.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael list /
  raphael list / -r --filter '{"type":"resource"}' --order-by updatedAt:desc --limit 4
  raphael list /work /personal --filter '{"tags":{"$in":["urgent"]}}'

Prints:
  One row per item: id, type, state, path, title. JSON carries `items` and
  `page`; paging notes go to stderr.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help addressing; raphael help output

---

Usage: raphael search <path>... -q <query> [options]
       raphael search --id <id> -q <query> [options]

Search all descendants by text.

Search is recursive by default; use --direct for direct children only. Search
everything with `raphael search / -q auth`. Matching includes titles,
descriptions and note text. Repeat -q to OR queries together, at most 10
queries. Each query allows at most 1000 characters and 32 words or quoted
phrases. Words separated by spaces mean OR; uppercase AND binds more tightly
than uppercase OR. Double quotes make a phrase. Words joined by punctuation,
such as auth-token or auth.token, must appear together and in that order, like a
phrase. * is punctuation too: auth* matches auth, not authentication. Operators
need a word or phrase on both sides. Lowercase and and or are ordinary search
words. There are no parentheses, NOT, wildcards or field prefixes. Matching
ignores case and accents; there is no stemming: token does not match tokens.

Results are ordered by relevance; --order-by is not accepted.

Rules:
  The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip
    skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one
    request.
  --filter accepts a JSON object with keys type, kind, tags. Allowed type
    values: area, project, resource. Allowed kind values: note. Each value is a
    scalar or {"$in":[...]}; sibling keys are ANDed. Tags match at least one
    supplied tag, with exact case after trimming and NFC normalization. A kind
    does not imply a type.
  A value that starts with -, such as a query, must be joined to its flag with
    =, as in `--query=-draft`; otherwise it is read as another option and
    refused.

Options:
      --id <id>
          Include this id alongside positional paths. Repeatable.
      --filter <json|@file>
          JSON object. Keys: type, kind, tags. Values may use {"$in":[...]}. See
          `raphael help addressing`.
      --skip <n>
          Number of results to skip. Default: 0.
      --limit <n>
          Page size, 1 to 500. Default: 50.
      --include-archived
          Include archived items.
  -q, --query <text>
          Text to search. At least one required. Repeatable.
      --direct
          Search direct children only.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael search / -q auth
  raphael search /work -q '"login flow" AND retry'
  raphael search /work /personal -q auth --limit 20
  raphael search /work --direct -q auth --filter '{"type":"resource","kind":"note"}'

Prints:
  One row per match, closest first: id, type, state, path, title. JSON carries
  `items[].node` and `page`.

Next: raphael get --id 42

See also: raphael help addressing; raphael help output

---

Usage: raphael status [--json]

Check the connection: which server, from where, and whether it accepts the key.

Uses the connection every remote command uses: RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and
RAPHAEL_API_KEY when both are set, otherwise the saved login.

Asks the server once whether the key is accepted and which protocol and release
it runs. Changes nothing.

Exit 0 means remote commands can work. Otherwise the exit code and error are the
ones the next read would meet: 5 for no connection, a rejected key, a protocol
mismatch, or an address that is not a Raphael server or does not speak HTTP; 6
when the server could not be reached or did not answer in time; 1 when the
server failed. A write that meets the same failure after it was sent exits 7,
because it may have been applied.

Options:
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael status
  raphael status --json
    The check an agent runs first.

Prints:
  The server address, its source, whether the key was accepted, and the protocol
  and release of each side. JSON has connection {endpoint, source, configFile},
  protocolVersion, serverVersion (null when the server does not say) and
  cliVersion.

Next: raphael list /

See also: raphael help connecting

---

Usage: raphael login [<address>] [--api-key-stdin] [--json]

Save a verified server address and API key on this machine.

Verifies the address and key with the server, then saves them with owner-only
permissions. Nothing is saved unless the server accepted them.

The address is the server's root, such as http://127.0.0.1:3000. The key is the
server's RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY.

Without an address argument, prompts for it. The key is read from a prompt that
does not show it, or with --api-key-stdin from standard input. It is never taken
as an argument.

Saved to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/raphael/config.json, or ~/.config/raphael/config.json,
where ~ is HOME, or the account's home directory when HOME is unset. Logging in
again replaces the saved login. When the address is different, login also says
which address it replaced.

RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY, when both are set, override the saved
login; login warns when they are set.

Prompts go to standard error. Cancelling exits 2 and saves nothing.

Rules:
  --api-key-stdin needs the address as an argument, and standard input must not
    be a terminal.
  The key on standard input is everything up to the end, with one trailing
    newline removed. It must be 1 to 512 visible ASCII characters with no
    spaces, on one line.

Options:
      --api-key-stdin
          Read the key from standard input instead of prompting.
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael login
    Prompt for the address and the key.
  raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000
    Prompt for the key only.
  raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000 --api-key-stdin --json
    Read the key from a pipe, as in:
    `printf '%s\n' "$KEY" | raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000 --api-key-stdin`

Prints:
  The verified address and the saved file. JSON has endpoint, protocolVersion,
  configFile and replaced: the previous address when it was different, otherwise
  null.

Next: raphael status

See also: raphael help connecting

---

Usage: raphael logout [--json]

Remove the saved login from this machine.

Deletes the saved login file after checking that its directory is yours and
private. Nothing on the server changes.

With no saved login, says so and exits 0.

RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY are not changed; when they are set,
commands keep using them, and logout warns.

Options:
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael logout
  raphael logout --json

Prints:
  What was removed, or that there was nothing to remove. JSON has removed,
  endpoint (null when unknown) and configFile.

Next: raphael login

See also: raphael help connecting

---

Usage: raphael server <subcommand> [options]

Manage the server on this machine.

Use `raphael server serve` to run the server.

Options:
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael server serve --help
  raphael help server serve
  raphael help connecting

Prints:
  Help for local server commands.

Next: raphael server serve --help

---

Usage: raphael server serve [--config <path>] [--host <address>] [--port <n>] [--database <path>]

Run a Raphael server on this machine.

The API key comes from RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY, in the environment or in a .env
file. With --config, .env is read from that file's directory; without it, from
the current directory.

Make an owner-only key file with:
`[ -e .env ] && echo ".env already exists; add RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY to it yourself." >&2 || (umask 077 && set -C && echo "RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > .env)`.
Clients log in with the same key. It must be 1 to 512 visible ASCII characters
with no spaces. In .env, quote a key that contains #, which otherwise starts a
comment.

Clients read RAPHAEL_API_KEY, not RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY, so starting a server
never changes which server client commands use.

Without --config, listens on 127.0.0.1:3000 with the database at
./data/raphael.sqlite, relative to the current directory.

The YAML file may set server.host, server.port and database.path; every key is
optional and unknown keys are refused. A relative database.path resolves against
the file's directory.

Flags override the file. A relative --database resolves against the current
directory.

The port is bound before the database is opened, so a port in use creates
nothing.

This command never reads your saved login. It prints only to standard error and
runs until Ctrl-C.

A stop by signal finishes the shutdown, then exits 130 after Ctrl-C (SIGINT) or
143 after SIGTERM. A second signal stops it at once, without finishing.

Options:
      --config <path>
          Read configuration from this YAML file.
      --host <address>
          Address to listen on. 0.0.0.0 listens on every interface. Default:
          127.0.0.1.
      --port <n>
          Port to listen on, from 1 to 65535. Default: 3000.
      --database <path>
          SQLite database file. Created if it does not exist. Default:
          ./data/raphael.sqlite.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael server serve
  raphael server serve --port 3100
  raphael server serve --config ./raphael.yaml
  raphael server serve --host 0.0.0.0 --database ./data/raphael.sqlite

Prints:
  The address, the database, where the key came from, and the login command, on
  standard error. Standard output is empty.

Next: raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000

See also: raphael help connecting

---

Usage: raphael version [--json]
       raphael --version [--json]

Print the installed Raphael release.

Works offline: reads no configuration and contacts no server.

To compare with a server, run `raphael status`, which reports both sides.

Options:
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael version
  raphael version --json
  raphael --version

Prints:
  The bare release number. JSON has version and protocolVersion.

Next: raphael status

---

Usage: raphael help [<command> | <topic>] [--json]

Read command help and cross-command guidance.

Commands take precedence over topics. Help works without a connection or saved
login. `raphael help <x>`, `raphael <x> --help` and `raphael <x> -h` print the
same page, as text or, with --json, as the same document.

`raphael help reference` prints every page in one stream.

Options:
      --json
          Print the result as one JSON document.
  -h, --help
          Show help.

Examples:
  raphael help create
  raphael help reference
  raphael help --json search

Prints:
  Help text. With --json, one document {"ok":true,"command":"help",...}:
  commands, topics, examples, exitCodes and errorCodes for the root page and
  reference, definition for a command, topic for a topic. `raphael help output`
  describes it.

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help addressing - Paths, ids, slugs, containment and field limits.

Paths are absolute: /work/raphael/backend. The root is /. For commands that
address one item, use a path or --id, never both. List and search may mix paths
and repeated --id. The root can be listed and searched, but cannot be read or
changed as an item. A path changes when an item or an ancestor moves or changes
slug; its positive integer id stays the same. --id is repeatable only on list
and search.

Types: area, project, resource.note. Items that can hold children: area,
project.

The root (/) can directly hold: area.
An area can directly hold: area, project, resource.note.
A project can directly hold: resource.note.
A resource.note cannot hold children.

NFC lowercase Unicode letter or number tokens joined by single hyphens;
combining marks may follow the first letter or number in each token. A slug may
contain at most 100 Unicode characters.

Create with a full path, or with --parent-id and --slug. The final path segment
is the new slug. Parent items must already exist. Renaming a title does not
change the slug; use `update --slug` to change its address. Archive retains the
item and reserves its slug. There is no delete command.

Titles are required and may contain at most 200 characters after trimming.
Descriptions may contain at most 4000 characters. Give each tag its own --tag:
1–50 characters per tag, at most 25 tags. Tags are trimmed and NFC-normalized,
are case-sensitive, and must not repeat or contain commas. Metadata is a JSON
object set once at create: at most 50 top-level keys, 5 levels deep, 100
characters per key, and 16384 serialized UTF-8 bytes.

The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip
skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one request.

For list: --order-by accepts slug, updatedAt, id with asc or desc; for example
`--order-by updatedAt:desc`. Search orders by relevance and does not accept
--order-by.

--filter accepts a JSON object with keys type, kind, tags. Allowed type values:
area, project, resource. Allowed kind values: note. Each value is a scalar or
{"$in":[...]}; sibling keys are ANDed. Tags match at least one supplied tag,
with exact case after trimming and NFC normalization. A kind does not imply a
type.

Examples:
  raphael get /work/raphael
  raphael get --id 42
  raphael create project --parent-id 42 --slug raphael --title Raphael

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help content - Note bodies, files, stdin and Markdown conversion.

Create a note with `create resource.note`. Without --title, a note takes its
title from the text of the body's first paragraph or heading, with its line
breaks read as spaces, then from the description's first line, cut at 200
characters. A title must still be available when the body is empty.

Body text is literal unless it starts with @: `--body @draft.md` reads a file,
and `--body @-` reads stdin. Use --body-literal to keep a leading @ as text. A
body read from stdin or a file that is empty or only whitespace is refused. To
explicitly clear an existing body use update with `--body ''` and --revision. A
pipe is read only when `--body @-` is given; on a terminal, @- says how to
finish typing.

A value that starts with -, such as a Markdown list, must be joined to its flag
with =, as in `--body='- first item'`; otherwise it is read as another option
and refused. A longer body is easier as a file or a pipe: `--body @list.md` or
`--body @-`.

Metadata and filters accept inline JSON or @file, but not @-. Metadata is set
once at create and cannot be updated. File and stdin reads are bounded, and
invalid independent arguments are refused before reading body content.

Body formats: markdown, tiptap. Supported Markdown heading levels: 1, 2, 3.
Supported document elements: doc, paragraph, text, heading, bulletList,
orderedList, listItem, codeBlock, blockquote, horizontalRule, hardBreak.
Supported text styles: bold, italic, strike, code, link. Documents allow at most
10000 elements, nested at most 64 levels deep counting each JSON object and
array, so about half that many levels of elements. The complete serialized
request may contain at most 1048576 UTF-8 bytes.

Markdown the stored document cannot represent is not stored as written: a table
is kept as Markdown source in a code block; a task list checkbox is kept as
plain text; an image is kept as plain text; raw HTML is kept as plain text; a
link whose destination is not allowed is kept as plain text; a link with no
label is left out; a link title is left out; a footnote is kept as plain text; a
heading deeper than level 3 (#### or more) is kept as Markdown source in a code
block. When that happens, create and update warn on standard error and in
warnings[]; --strict refuses the body instead, with exit 2. Escaping, joined
lines and other rewrites that keep the meaning are not reported. Text such as
List<String> is an HTML tag by CommonMark's rules; put it in backticks to keep
it as code.

A body that is a TipTap JSON document must be sent with `--body-format tiptap`;
sent as Markdown it is refused. To keep JSON as note text, put it in a fenced
code block. Markdown output is a best-effort export of the stored document; use
`--json --return-format tiptap` to read its structured body. --return-format
requires --json; it controls the response, while --body-format controls
submitted content.

Examples:
  raphael create resource.note /work/idea --body '# An idea'
  raphael get /work/idea --json --return-format tiptap
  raphael update /work/idea --body '' --revision 3

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help revisions - Safe writes, conflicts, archives and unknown outcomes.

A revision is a positive integer returned when an item is read. Update, move,
archive and restore check it before changing anything. Pass --revision to apply
a change only to the version you read. For changes other than body replacements,
omitting --revision makes the CLI read the current revision first and pin the
subsequent write to that same id. Body replacements require an explicit
--revision.

Exit 4 means stored state prevented the change and nothing was applied. For a
revision conflict, run get again, compare your intended change with the current
item, then retry with its new revision. Do not blindly replace the revision and
overwrite a concurrent edit. A slug conflict can also be caused by an archived
item: choose another slug or restore the existing item.

Archiving an item hides it and its descendants from ordinary lists and search.
--include-archived includes active and archived items. Archived items retain
their slugs. Restore removes only the cause archive put on the item itself; an
archived ancestor, or a cause set by something other than archive, keeps the
item archived. Read it again and restore the blocking ancestor when appropriate.

Exit 7 means a write was sent and its outcome is unknown. Read the item to
reconcile before deciding whether to try again. For an uncertain create, read
the intended path; retrying create may encounter your own slug collision. Exit 6
means retrying may work and the change was not applied.

Examples:
  raphael get /work/idea --json
  raphael update /work/idea --title 'Revised idea' --revision 3
  raphael list /work --include-archived

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help output - Text results, JSON envelopes, paging and diagnostics.

Standard output holds results only. Without --json, lists, search and favorites
print one row per item: id, type, state, path and title, in columns padded to
the widest value on the page. State is -, active, archived or active,archived,
and comes before the title, which is always last. A header line is printed only
on a terminal. A write prints a line naming the item, then its path, and its
revision when it has one; a write that changed nothing says Unchanged; get
prints every field, including the update time. List order defaults to slug
ascending; use `list --order-by updatedAt:desc` for recent changes. Search
orders by relevance and does not accept --order-by. Bodies are printed as
Markdown by default.

With --json, a successful command prints exactly one document:
{"ok":true,"command":"get",...data}. Full-item reads and writes use entity;
move, archive and restore use node; lists and favorites use items; search uses
items[].node. These are machine field names. Items include path and updatedAt,
an ISO 8601 UTC time such as 2026-10-19T10:42:07.123Z; text output prints the
same string. No createdAt field is exposed. Update, move, archive, restore,
favorite and unfavorite include changed; false means success without a change
and without a revision bump. Move and update also report previousPath, the path
before the change; text output prints it as was: only when the path changed.
Favorite commands report `nodeId`, `isFavorite` and `node`, which is null when
unfavorite names an id that does not exist. Path reports path.

Paged results include page with skip, limit, hasMore, nextSkip and
archivedLeftOut, which is true when archived items were left out of the answer.
The result footer and runnable next-page command go to standard error, never
standard output. Empty-result notes, warnings, hints, prompts and failures also
go to standard error. Warnings appear in warnings[] on JSON successes as
{code, message}, plus constructs for markdown_degraded. Codes:
durability_unconfirmed: login saved the key, but the filesystem did not confirm
the write is durable. environment_overrides: login saved the key, but
RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT or RAPHAEL_API_KEY is set: with both, remote commands use them
instead; with one alone, remote commands refuse to run. environment_still_set:
logout removed the saved login, but RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT or RAPHAEL_API_KEY is still
set: with both, remote commands use them; with one alone, remote commands refuse
to run. version_skew: status found a server of another release that speaks the
same protocol. markdown_degraded: create or update did not store some Markdown
as written; constructs lists them by name. Closing a pipe early does not turn a
completed command into a failure.

JSON failures leave stdout empty and print exactly one document to stderr:
{"ok":false,"command":"get","error":{...}}. The only other text stderr can then
hold is for a person at a terminal: login's prompts, and the note that --body @-
is reading from one. command is the command as typed (help for every help form),
or null when none was recognized. error always has code, category, message,
hint, exitCode, applied and retryable. Optional fields include status, field,
target, serverMessage, details, and connection {endpoint, source, configFile}
once a connection was resolved. category is the word for exitCode: failed for 1,
usage for 2, not_found for 3, conflict for 4, not_configured for 5, retryable
for 6, outcome_unknown for 7, interrupted for 130 and 143. code is a server code
or one of this CLI's: usage, cancelled, interrupted, invalid_request,
unexpected, not_configured, unsupported_platform, no_home,
relative_xdg_config_home, relative_home, directory_unusable,
directory_uncreatable, file_unusable, file_unreadable, file_too_large,
file_malformed, write_failed, incomplete_environment, malformed,
unsupported_scheme, credentials_in_url, query_not_allowed, fragment_not_allowed,
api_key_missing, api_key_unsendable, timeout_out_of_range, unreachable,
blocked_port, not_http, connection_lost, timeout, incompatible_protocol,
not_raphael, bad_response, http_status, port_in_use, listen_denied,
host_unavailable, listen_failed, database_in_use, database_newer,
database_unusable, start_failed, option_invalid, config_unreadable,
config_too_large, config_malformed, config_unsafe, config_invalid,
env_unreadable, env_too_large; `raphael help exit-codes` gives each one's exit
codes and meaning. For a server's answer, details carries what the server
reported, each field optional: field, index, reason, limit, currentRevision,
blocking, parentType, childType, location, element; blocking is {id, type}. For
incompatible_protocol, details is
{serverProtocol, serverVersion, cliProtocol, cliVersion}, with null for what the
server did not say, and serverCode when its answer carried a code of its own.
applied is false when no change was applied, true when known applied, and null
when unknown. Retry decisions must consider exitCode and applied; do not blindly
retry an uncertain write. The JSON envelope and exit-code meanings are stable
within a major version.

help --json prints one document to stdout: {"ok":true,"command":"help",...}. For
the root page and for reference it has commands, topics, examples, exitCodes and
errorCodes; for a command, definition; for a topic, topic. A definition has
name, summary, group, writes, usage, behavior, options, examples, prints and
next, and may have constraints and seeAlso. Each option has name, type and
description, and may have short, multiple, whole {min, max}, valueName, default
and constraint. A topic has name, summary, body and examples. Each errorCodes
entry has code, source, exitCodes and categories, and a meaning for this CLI's
codes.

For text write failures, the state line distinguishes Nothing was changed., The
change was not sent., and The change may or may not have been applied. Set
RAPHAEL_DEBUG=1 to include diagnostic stacks. Keep stdout for results when
scripting.

On standard error, a line that starts with hint: is a runnable next step, and a
line that starts with warning: is something a success did not do as asked. Other
lines are information only, such as Showing 1-3. or Nothing changed: it was
already archived. A note that also suggests a command puts that command on its
own hint: line. A hint that is a command quotes all of it, as in hint: Run
"raphael favorites" to see your favorites. or hint: Did you mean
"raphael list /work"?

Archive causes print one per line, nearest first: directly, or through the
archived container above the item, then who set the cause in parentheses. (user)
is a cause set with archive; restore removes only that cause, and only on the
item itself. JSON carries each cause as origin, owner and reason.

The default page contains at most 50 results. --limit accepts 1–500; --skip
skips results. At most 10 paths or ids may be searched or listed in one request.

For list: --order-by accepts slug, updatedAt, id with asc or desc; for example
`--order-by updatedAt:desc`. Search orders by relevance and does not accept
--order-by.

Examples:
  raphael list / --json
  raphael list / --json --order-by updatedAt:desc
  raphael get --id 42 --json --return-format tiptap

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help connecting - Start a local server and connect a client.

Run a server: put RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=<key> in an owner-only .env file, made
with
`[ -e .env ] && echo ".env already exists; add RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY to it yourself." >&2 || (umask 077 && set -C && echo "RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY=$(openssl rand -hex 32)" > .env)`,
then run `raphael server serve` in that directory. With --config, the .env
beside the configuration file is read instead. An environment value wins over
.env, and an empty one is refused rather than ignored. The server listens on
http://127.0.0.1:3000 unless --host, --port or the configuration file say
otherwise, and prints the address and the login command when it starts. It never
reads your saved login.

Connect: in another terminal run `raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000` and enter
the same key. Login verifies the address and key before saving them with
owner-only permissions to $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/raphael/config.json, or
~/.config/raphael/config.json. Without a terminal, pipe the key:
`printf '%s\n' "$KEY" | raphael login <address> --api-key-stdin`. Logging in
again replaces the saved login; `raphael logout` removes it.

Check: `raphael status` reports the server, where its address and key came from,
and the protocol and release of each side. Run it first in a script; exit 0
means remote commands can work.

Without a saved login, set RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY together. Both
or neither: a partial pair is refused, an empty variable counts as unset, and a
complete pair overrides the saved login. RAPHAEL_API_KEY is the server's
RAPHAEL_SERVER_API_KEY; clients do not read .env, and the server does not read
RAPHAEL_API_KEY. A key must be 1 to 512 visible ASCII characters with no spaces.

RAPHAEL_TIMEOUT sets the request timeout in whole seconds, from 1 to 120; the
default is 15 seconds. Exit 5 means the connection needs attention: the error
names the address and where it came from, and the hint says what to change. Exit
6 means the server could not be reached and nothing was applied. Read
`raphael help exit-codes` before retrying a failed write.

Examples:
  raphael server serve
  raphael login http://127.0.0.1:3000
  raphael status --json

Next: raphael help reference

---

raphael help exit-codes - Choose recovery from the process exit code.

  0    Done. This includes no-ops and success with warnings.
  1    Failed for another reason, with nothing left uncertain.
  2    The command as typed can never succeed; nothing was applied.
  3    Not found.
  4    Conflicts with stored state; nothing was applied.
  5    Not configured, or not authorized.
  6    Not applied, and retrying may work.
  7    Outcome unknown: a write was sent and no definite answer came back.
  130  Interrupted by SIGINT.
  143  Interrupted by SIGTERM.

For exit 7 reconcile by reading before retrying. SIGINT and SIGTERM retain their
conventional codes; a write interrupted after sending may still have been
applied. JSON failures also report applied and retryable.

Every failure carries a stable identifier in error.code. A failure the server
answered keeps the server's code; every other failure carries one of this CLI's
codes. A code listed nowhere here comes from a newer server, and its exitCode
still applies. Choose a recovery from exitCode; use the code to tell causes
apart.

Server codes:
  invalid_input             Exit 2.
  unauthorized              Exit 5.
  node_not_found            Exit 3.
  route_not_found           Exit 5.
  method_not_allowed        Exit 5.
  slug_conflict             Exit 4.
  revision_conflict         Exit 4.
  node_archived             Exit 4.
  payload_too_large         Exit 2.
  unsupported_media_type    Exit 2.
  invalid_parent            Exit 4.
  unsupported_content       Exit 2.
  storage_busy              Exit 6.
  internal_error            Exit 1, or 7 when a write may have been applied.

Codes from this CLI:
  usage                     Exit 2. The command as typed was refused before
                            anything was sent: an unknown command or option, a
                            missing or malformed value, or input the rules
                            refuse.
  cancelled                 Exit 2. A login prompt was cancelled; nothing was
                            saved.
  interrupted               Exit 130 or 143. SIGINT or SIGTERM stopped the
                            command. After a write was sent, applied is null:
                            read before retrying.
  invalid_request           Exit 2. The request failed the client's own check,
                            so it was not sent.
  unexpected                Exit 1 or 7. A fault in this CLI. Exit 7 when a
                            write had already been sent, otherwise 1. Run it
                            again with RAPHAEL_DEBUG=1 to see where it failed.
  not_configured            Exit 5. There is no saved login, and
                            RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY are not set.
  unsupported_platform      Exit 5. Saved logins are not supported on this
                            platform; set RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and RAPHAEL_API_KEY
                            instead.
  no_home                   Exit 5. XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set, and HOME is
                            empty, or unset on an account with no home
                            directory, so there is no place for a saved login.
                            An unset HOME otherwise falls back to the account's
                            home directory.
  relative_xdg_config_home  Exit 5. XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not an absolute path.
  relative_home             Exit 5. HOME is not an absolute path.
  directory_unusable        Exit 5. The directory for the saved login, or a
                            directory above it, cannot be inspected, is not a
                            directory, or is not private to you.
  directory_uncreatable     Exit 5. The directory for the saved login could not
                            be created.
  file_unusable             Exit 5. The saved login file is a link, not a
                            regular file, or readable by other users.
  file_unreadable           Exit 5. The saved login file could not be read.
  file_too_large            Exit 5. The saved login file is larger than a saved
                            login can be.
  file_malformed            Exit 5. The saved login file is not JSON with an
                            endpoint and an API key.
  write_failed              Exit 5. login could not save, or logout could not
                            remove, the saved login file.
  incomplete_environment    Exit 5. Only one of RAPHAEL_ENDPOINT and
                            RAPHAEL_API_KEY is set; set both or neither.
  malformed                 Exit 2 or 5. The server address is not a URL. Exit 2
                            for an address typed at login, 5 otherwise.
  unsupported_scheme        Exit 2 or 5. The server address does not start with
                            http:// or https://. Exit 2 for an address typed at
                            login, 5 otherwise.
  credentials_in_url        Exit 2 or 5. The server address carries a user name
                            or password. Exit 2 for an address typed at login, 5
                            otherwise.
  query_not_allowed         Exit 2 or 5. The server address has a part after
                            "?". Exit 2 for an address typed at login, 5
                            otherwise.
  fragment_not_allowed      Exit 2 or 5. The server address has a part after
                            "#". Exit 2 for an address typed at login, 5
                            otherwise.
  api_key_missing           Exit 2 or 5. No API key: none was given at login
                            (exit 2), the saved login has none, or the server
                            has none configured (exit 5).
  api_key_unsendable        Exit 2 or 5. The API key cannot be sent in a request
                            header. Exit 2 for a key given at login, 5 for a
                            saved, environment or server key.
  timeout_out_of_range      Exit 5. RAPHAEL_TIMEOUT is not a whole number of
                            seconds in the allowed range.
  unreachable               Exit 5 or 6. Could not connect to the server, so
                            nothing was sent. Exit 5 when the secure connection
                            could not be set up or the server's certificate is
                            not trusted, which retrying cannot change; otherwise
                            6.
  blocked_port              Exit 5. The address uses a port that requests are
                            never sent to, so nothing was sent.
  not_http                  Exit 5 or 7. Something answered at the address, but
                            not in HTTP. Exit 7 when a write may have been sent,
                            otherwise 5.
  connection_lost           Exit 5, 6 or 7. The connection failed after the
                            request was sent. Exit 7 for a write, 5 when the
                            secure connection failed, otherwise 6.
  timeout                   Exit 6 or 7. No answer within the timeout
                            (RAPHAEL_TIMEOUT). Exit 7 when a write may have been
                            sent, otherwise 6.
  incompatible_protocol     Exit 5 or 7. The server speaks another protocol
                            version; details names both sides. Exit 7 when a
                            write may have been applied, otherwise 5; after a
                            write answered with a redirect or a bare 3xx or 4xx
                            status, applied is null with exit 5.
  not_raphael               Exit 5 or 7. The address does not answer as a
                            Raphael server. Exit 7 when a write may have been
                            applied, otherwise 5; after a write answered with a
                            redirect or a bare 3xx or 4xx status, applied is
                            null with exit 5.
  bad_response              Exit 1, 5 or 7. The answer could not be read, or was
                            a redirect or a status the command does not expect.
                            Exit 7 for an unreadable success after a write, 1
                            for one on a read, otherwise 5; after a write,
                            applied is null, because something in front of the
                            server may have forwarded it.
  http_status               Exit 1, 5 or 7. The answer was a bare HTTP status
                            with no Raphael error, often from a proxy. For a 5xx
                            status: exit 7 after a write, 1 on a read. Otherwise
                            5; after a write, applied is null, because a proxy
                            may have forwarded it.
  port_in_use               Exit 5. The port is already in use.
  listen_denied             Exit 5. This user may not listen on that port.
  host_unavailable          Exit 5. The host is not an address on this machine.
  listen_failed             Exit 1. The server could not listen for another
                            reason the error names.
  database_in_use           Exit 5. Another process, such as another Raphael
                            server, is using the database.
  database_newer            Exit 5. The database was migrated by a newer or
                            different Raphael release.
  database_unusable         Exit 1 or 5. The database could not be opened or
                            created. Exit 1 when the cause is not a setting,
                            such as a broken installation, otherwise 5.
  start_failed              Exit 1. The server could not start for a reason of
                            its own.
  option_invalid            Exit 2. A server serve flag has a value the server
                            cannot use.
  config_unreadable         Exit 5. The server configuration file could not be
                            read.
  config_too_large          Exit 5. The server configuration file is too large.
  config_malformed          Exit 5. The server configuration file is empty or
                            not valid YAML.
  config_unsafe             Exit 5. The server configuration file uses YAML that
                            is not read literally: an anchor, an alias, a tag,
                            or more than one document.
  config_invalid            Exit 5. A server setting has a value the server
                            cannot use.
  env_unreadable            Exit 5. The .env file the server reads could not be
                            read.
  env_too_large             Exit 5. The .env file the server reads is too large.

Examples:
  raphael get /work --json
  raphael help revisions
  raphael help output

Next: raphael help reference
==== stderr ====
